  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes where major lithium brine resources are located and how lithium is extracted from them. A world map highlights several important sites, including Clayton Valley in Nevada, salt-lake deposits in Tibet/China, and South American salars in the “lithium triangle.” A zoomed inset over northern Chile and Argentina emphasizes especially large production at Atacama, along with Hombre Muerto and Uyuni, with annual output shown in tonnes of lithium carbonate equivalent. Geothermal deposits in Europe are also marked, suggesting a broader global distribution beyond salt flats.\n\nThe lower illustration explains the brine-extraction process in an arid basin. Brine is pumped from a deep saline aquifer to a series of solar evaporation ponds. Under strong sunlight, water evaporates and the lithium chloride concentration increases as the brine moves through successive ponds. Waste salts appear to be removed along the way, and reagents are added at a processing plant to recover the final product, labeled lithium carbonate. The cross-section also shows a separate freshwater aquifer above or beside the saline system, underscoring the hydrogeological setting of extraction in desert salt flats.",
